SCHEDULER PORTING ROADMAP
Porting lyre's scheduler/process/thread model to vex
=======================================================

SOURCE: ext/lyre/sched/, ext/lyre/sys/, ext/lyre/lib/, ext/lyre/dev/
TARGET: arch-agnostic design (arch/amd64/ for arch-specific, kernel/ + sched/ for generic)

Overview
--------
Lyre implements a preemptive round-robin SMP scheduler backed by a global lock-free
run queue (CAS array, 65536 slots). Threads are first-class; processes are address-space
containers. Context switches are driven by per-CPU LAPIC one-shot timers. Idle CPUs
sleep and are woken via IPIs when new threads become runnable.

The vex kernel already has:
  - hart abstraction (hart_local, GS-based per-CPU, hart.c / hart.h)
  - initcall phases (SMP phases: hart, arch, ireq, syscall, timer, late)
  - LAPIC + IPI infrastructure (lapic.c)
  - IRQ dispatch framework (ireq.c / intctl.c)
  - VMM with pagemap (vmm.c)
  - Spinlock + atomics (spinlock.h / atomic.h)

What is missing and needs to be built:
  - Thread and process data structures
  - Arch-agnostic scheduler core
  - Arch-specific context save/restore
  - LAPIC timer-driven preemption wired to scheduler
  - FPU/extended state save/restore
  - Event (blocking synchronization) primitive
  - Kernel thread + user thread creation APIs
  - syscall entry wired to thread context

=======================================================
STAGE 1: Thread Control Block & Per-CPU Wiring
=======================================================

Goal: Define the thread struct and wire it into hart_local.

Files to create/modify:
  include/vex/thread.h          -- arch-agnostic thread struct
  include/vex/process.h         -- arch-agnostic process struct
  arch/amd64/include/vex.arch/thread.h  -- amd64 arch-specific thread fields
  arch/amd64/kernel/thread.c   -- amd64 thread init (GS, MSRs, FPU storage)
  arch/amd64/include/vex.arch/hart.h    -- extend hart_local to hold current_thread ptr

Thread struct (arch-agnostic part, include/vex/thread.h):
  - tid, state (enum: RUNNABLE, RUNNING, BLOCKED, DEAD)
  - pointer to owning process
  - int timeslice_us (default 5000)
  - spinlock_t lock
  - kernel_stack pointer
  - arch_thread_t arch  -- opaque arch-specific block (defined per arch)
  - event synchronization fields (stage 5)

Arch-specific block for amd64 (arch/vex.arch/thread.h):
  - struct cpu_ctx ctx      -- saved GP registers (already defined in idts.S frame layout)
  - uint64_t cr3
  - void *gs_base, *fs_base
  - void *fpu_storage
  - void *pf_stack           -- dedicated page-fault IST stack

hart_local extension:
  - struct thread *current_thread  -- pointer to currently running thread
  - struct thread *idle_thread     -- idle thread for this hart

Use hart_local.current_thread instead of lyre's GS:0x0 thread-pointer trick.
(vex already uses GS for hart_local; thread pointer lives inside hart_local.)

Acceptance: hart_local has a current_thread field; thread struct compiles; arch fields
are hidden behind arch_thread_t typedef.

=======================================================
STAGE 2: Run Queue & Scheduler Core
=======================================================

Goal: Implement a global lock-free run queue and basic round-robin selection.

Files to create:
  kernel/sched/sched.c          -- scheduler core
  include/vex/sched.h           -- public scheduler API

Design (closely following lyre, adapted for vex conventions):

  #define SCHED_QUEUE_SIZE 65536
  static struct thread *run_queue[SCHED_QUEUE_SIZE];

  Enqueue: CAS-loop over run_queue[], find first NULL slot, atomically claim it.
  Dequeue: linear scan with CAS; used when a thread is blocked or dies.
  get_next_thread(hart): round-robin scan from hart->last_queue_idx + 1,
    try spinlock_tryacquire on each candidate, skip locked threads.

Public API:
  void sched_enqueue(struct thread *t);
  void sched_dequeue(struct thread *t);
  void sched_yield(void);           -- cooperative yield
  noreturn void sched_dequeue_and_die(void);

sched_entry(struct cpu_ctx *ctx):
  -- called from timer IRQ handler and IPI handler
  1. Get current hart; get current_thread.
  2. If current_thread is not idle and not DEAD: save ctx into thread->arch.ctx,
     save CR3, GS/FS bases, FPU state; set state = RUNNABLE; enqueue back.
  3. Call get_next_thread(); if NULL -> run idle thread.
  4. Set hart->current_thread = next; set state = RUNNING.
  5. Restore arch state (CR3, GS/FS, FPU, kernel stack into TSS).
  6. Call arch_thread_spinup(&next->arch.ctx)  -- noreturn, does iretq.

Wire into initcall phase "multicore" (BSP) and "hart" (SMP).

Acceptance: kernel compiles; sched_entry is reachable from timer IRQ path.

=======================================================
STAGE 3: LAPIC Timer Preemption
=======================================================

Goal: Drive preemption via per-CPU LAPIC one-shot timer.

Context: lapic_timer.c exists but is commented out / disabled.

Files to modify/create:
  arch/amd64/kernel/lapic_timer.c  -- implement lapic_timer_calibrate() and
                                       lapic_timer_oneshot(uint64_t us, uint8_t vec)
  arch/amd64/kernel/lapic.c        -- expose lapic_send_ipi() if not already public
  kernel/sched/sched.c              -- call lapic_timer_oneshot at end of sched_entry

Calibration (follow lyre's approach):
  1. Use HPET or PIT as reference clock (vex has both: hpet.c, arch uacpi).
  2. Disable LAPIC timer; read HPET counter T0.
  3. Program LAPIC timer with known initial count.
  4. Spin for known real-time interval (e.g. 10 ms via HPET).
  5. Read remaining LAPIC count; compute ticks/us.
  6. Store in hart_local.lapic_freq_per_us.

lapic_timer_oneshot(us, vector):
  lapic_write(LVT_TIMER, vector | LAPIC_TIMER_ONE_SHOT);
  lapic_write(TIMER_DIV, 0);
  lapic_write(TIMER_INITCNT, us * hart->lapic_freq_per_us);

Register a dedicated IRQ vector (e.g. 0x40) for the scheduler; handler calls sched_entry.
Also register a second vector (e.g. 0x41) for scheduler IPI (wake idle CPUs).

At end of sched_entry, before spinup: arm the one-shot timer for next thread's timeslice.

Idle loop:
  void idle_thread_fn(void) {
      for (;;) { sti; hlt; }  -- wait for IPI from sched_enqueue
  }

On sched_enqueue, if any hart is running its idle thread: send IPI to wake it.

Acceptance: booting with 2+ CPUs, both CPUs cycle through kernel threads visibly.

=======================================================
STAGE 4: Arch Context Switch (amd64)
=======================================================

Goal: Correct save/restore of full CPU state including FPU.

Files to create/modify:
  arch/amd64/kernel/thread.c   -- arch_thread_init(), arch_thread_spinup()
  arch/amd64/kernel/fpu.c      -- fpu_init(), fpu_save(), fpu_restore()
  arch/amd64/include/vex.arch/thread.h

arch_thread_init(struct thread *t, void *entry, void *stack, bool user):
  - Zero ctx.
  - Set ctx.rip = entry, ctx.rsp = stack top.
  - Set ctx.cs = user ? 0x4b : 0x08; ctx.ss = user ? 0x53 : 0x10.
  - Set ctx.rflags = 0x202 (IF set).
  - Allocate fpu_storage (XSAVE area or 512B FXSAVE area).
  - If user: set cr3 from process pagemap; allocate pf_stack (IST2).
  - If kernel: cr3 = sys_pagemap->cr3.

arch_thread_spinup(struct cpu_ctx *ctx):   -- noreturn
  -- identical to lyre's thread_spinup():
  asm volatile (
      "mov %0, %%rsp\n\t"
      ... pop all regs ...
      "add $8, %%rsp\n\t"   // skip error code slot
      "swapgs\n\t"          // only if returning to user (check CS before call)
      "iretq"
      :: "rm"(ctx) : "memory"
  );

  Caller must decide swapgs based on ctx->cs before calling.
  Alternative: always swapgs in spinup, but set up GS correctly beforehand.

FPU handling:
  - On first use of FPU by a thread: catch #NM (Device Not Available, int 7),
    then fpu_restore for that thread (lazy FPU switching).
  - Or: always save/restore on every context switch (eager, simpler, lyre's approach).
  - Recommend eager for now; switch to lazy later.
  - fpu_init(): CPUID check for XSAVE; set CR4.OSXSAVE if supported.
  - fpu_save(void *storage): xsave or fxsave.
  - fpu_restore(void *storage): xrstor or fxrstor.

Acceptance: kernel threads context-switch correctly; no register corruption.

=======================================================
STAGE 5: Event / Blocking Synchronization
=======================================================

Goal: Threads can block waiting for an event; other threads/IRQ handlers trigger it.

Files to create:
  include/vex/event.h
  kernel/event.c

Port lyre's lib/event.c directly. It is already arch-agnostic; only dependency is
sched_enqueue / sched_dequeue and spinlock.

struct event {
    spinlock_t lock;
    size_t pending;
    size_t listeners_i;
    struct event_listener listeners[EVENT_MAX_LISTENERS];  -- 32 or 64
};

struct event_listener {
    struct thread *thread;
    size_t which;   -- distinguishes which event woke a multi-event wait
};

API:
  void event_trigger(struct event *e, bool from_irq);
  ssize_t event_await(struct event **events, size_t count, bool block);

event_await():
  1. Check pending on each event; if any > 0 return immediately.
  2. Attach self as listener on each event.
  3. sched_dequeue(current_thread); set state = BLOCKED.
  4. sched_yield();  -- switch away
  5. On resume: return which_event.

event_trigger():
  1. Enqueue all listening threads.
  2. Clear listener list.
  3. If from_irq and any idle hart exists: send IPI.

This primitive is sufficient to implement: process wait, device I/O completion,
keyboard input, timer sleep, futex.

Acceptance: one thread can block on an event; another thread triggers it and wakes it.

=======================================================
STAGE 6: Process Creation & Address Space
=======================================================

Goal: Create and destroy processes with independent virtual address spaces.

Files to create:
  include/vex/process.h
  kernel/sched/process.c

struct process {
    int pid;
    int ppid;
    struct pagemap *pagemap;
    uintptr_t mmap_anon_base;     -- start for anonymous mmap (e.g. 0x0000800000000000)
    uintptr_t thread_stack_top;   -- user stack allocation pointer
    spinlock_t lock;
    struct thread **threads;      -- dynamic array or linked list
    struct process **children;
    struct event event;           -- for waitpid
    struct vfs_node *cwd;
    struct file_descriptor *fds[MAX_FDS];
    spinlock_t fds_lock;
    char name[128];
};

process_create(const char *name, struct process *parent):
  - Allocate struct process.
  - vmm_fork(parent->pagemap) or create fresh pagemap.
  - Assign PID (atomic counter).
  - Set ppid, cwd, inherit fds from parent if fork.
  - Return process pointer.

process_destroy(struct process *p):
  - Kill and wait for all threads.
  - vmm_destroy(p->pagemap).
  - Close all file descriptors.
  - Trigger p->event to wake waiters.

PID 0 reserved (kernel/idle), PID 1 = init.

Acceptance: kernel can create a process and associate threads to it.

=======================================================
STAGE 7: User Thread Creation & Stack Layout
=======================================================

Goal: Create user-mode threads with proper stack setup (argv, envp, auxv).

Files to create:
  kernel/sched/uthread.c    -- user thread creation helpers

sched_new_user_thread(struct process *proc, void *entry, void *arg):
  1. Allocate kernel stack (e.g. 64 KB): vmm_alloc_kernel(KERNEL_STACK_SIZE).
  2. Allocate user stack (e.g. 64 KB): vmm_alloc_user(proc->pagemap, USER_STACK_SIZE).
     Decrement proc->thread_stack_top by stack size + guard page.
  3. Allocate page-fault IST stack (8 KB), wire into TSS IST2.
  4. arch_thread_init(t, entry, user_stack_top, true).
  5. Enqueue thread.

argv/envp/auxv layout on user stack:
  Follow System V AMD64 ABI:
  [argc][argv ptrs...][NULL][envp ptrs...][NULL][auxv pairs...][AT_NULL]
  Push strings first, then build pointer arrays downward.

Acceptance: a user-mode thread can be created and reaches its entry point.

=======================================================
STAGE 8: Syscall Entry Wired to Thread Context
=======================================================

Goal: syscall entry saves/restores thread context correctly; syscalls can block.

Files to modify/create:
  arch/amd64/kernel/syscall.S  -- syscall entry stub (SYSCALL instruction or sysenter)
  arch/amd64/kernel/syscall.c  -- syscall dispatch table
  kernel/syscall/syscall.c      -- arch-agnostic syscall dispatch

Syscall entry (SYSCALL path, preferred on amd64):
  1. swapgs  -- switch to kernel GS (hart_local)
  2. Save user RSP; load kernel stack from hart_local->current_thread->arch.kernel_stack
  3. Push synthetic cpu_ctx frame (RIP=RCX, RFLAGS=R11, RSP=saved user RSP)
  4. Push all GPRs
  5. Call syscall_dispatch(nr, ctx)
  6. Pop GPRs; restore RSP; swapgs; sysretq

syscall_dispatch must be able to:
  - Block the current thread (call sched_yield after event_await).
  - Return -EINTR if interrupted by signal (future).

Minimum syscalls for basic userspace:
  exit, fork (or clone), exec, waitpid, read, write, open, close, mmap, munmap.

Acceptance: a user thread can invoke a syscall; kernel handles it and returns.

=======================================================
STAGE 9: Integration & Testing
=======================================================

Tasks:
  - Wire sched initcall into BSP "multicore" phase:
      sched_init(): create idle threads for all harts, arm LAPIC timer on BSP.
  - Wire sched initcall into SMP "hart" phase:
      sched_ap_init(): create idle thread for this AP, arm LAPIC timer.
  - Create a test kernel thread in kmain that prints "tick N" every second
    using a timer event, proving preemption and event-blocking work.
  - Stress test: spawn N kernel threads, verify all run (no starvation).
  - Verify panic IPI (existing in lapic.c) still halts all harts while scheduler runs.

=======================================================
DEPENDENCY GRAPH
=======================================================

  [Stage 1: Thread/Process structs + hart_local wiring]
      |
      v
  [Stage 2: Run queue + sched_entry skeleton]
      |
      v
  [Stage 3: LAPIC timer calibration + preemption]  <-- needs Stage 2
  [Stage 4: Arch context switch + FPU]             <-- needs Stage 1
      |           |
      v           v
  [Stage 5: Events / blocking]                     <-- needs Stage 2
  [Stage 6: Process + address space]               <-- needs Stage 1, VMM
      |
      v
  [Stage 7: User thread creation + stack layout]   <-- needs Stage 4, 6
      |
      v
  [Stage 8: Syscall entry]                         <-- needs Stage 7
      |
      v
  [Stage 9: Integration + testing]

Stages 3 and 4 can proceed in parallel after Stage 2.
Stages 5 and 6 can proceed in parallel after Stage 2.

=======================================================
KEY DIFFERENCES FROM LYRE (THINGS TO ADAPT)
=======================================================

1. Per-CPU state access:
   Lyre: GS:0x0 == current thread pointer.
   Vex:  GS == hart_local; current thread at hart_local->current_thread.
   Impact: sched_current_thread() reads this_hart()->current_thread instead
   of a direct GS load. Slightly more indirection, no functional change.

2. Interrupt vector allocation:
   Lyre: hardcodes sched_vector.
   Vex:  use add_ireq_handler() to register the scheduler IRQ dynamically,
   or reserve a dedicated vector in ireq.h (e.g. IREQ_SCHED = 0x40).

3. Initcall integration:
   Lyre: manual call order in main().
   Vex:  use initcall sections. sched_init registers as INITCALL_MULTICORE;
   per-AP setup registers as INITCALL_SMP_HART.

4. Spinlock API:
   Lyre: spinlock_acquire / spinlock_release.
   Vex:  spinlock_acquire / spinlock_release already exist in spinlock.h.
   API is compatible; just rename if needed.

5. Pagemap / VMM:
   Lyre: has its own vmm with pagemap_fork.
   Vex:  vmm.c exists with sys_pagemap; need to implement vmm_fork() for processes.
   This is a prerequisite for Stage 6.

6. No process concept yet in lyre's scheduler per se - process is just an owner of
   a pagemap and file descriptors. The scheduler operates purely on threads.
   Vex should follow the same separation: scheduler owns threads, not processes.

=======================================================
WHAT TO COPY VERBATIM (WITH MINOR ADAPTATIONS)
=======================================================

  ext/lyre/sched/sched.c        -- run queue, get_next_thread, sched_entry, enqueue/dequeue
  ext/lyre/lib/event.c          -- event_trigger, event_await (replace lyre spinlock with vex)
  ext/lyre/sys/cpu.c            -- FPU detection, xsave area size calculation
  ext/lyre/sched/sched.c:74     -- thread_spinup() assembly (arch/amd64/kernel/thread.c)

WHAT TO REWRITE FROM SCRATCH (lyre version too tightly coupled):
  CPU init (already done in vex as hart.c + smp.c)
  LAPIC init (already done in vex as lapic.c)
  IDT / ISR (already done in vex)
  GDT / TSS (already done in vex as gdt.c)
  Syscall entry (vex will have its own conventions)

=======================================================
ESTIMATED EFFORT PER STAGE
=======================================================

Stage 1:  small    -- struct definitions, extending hart_local
Stage 2:  medium   -- run queue + sched_entry logic
Stage 3:  medium   -- LAPIC timer calibration (finicky but well-understood)
Stage 4:  medium   -- assembly spinup + FPU (careful, architecture-sensitive)
Stage 5:  small    -- straight port of event.c
Stage 6:  small    -- process struct + creation/destruction
Stage 7:  medium   -- stack layout details (ABI compliance matters)
Stage 8:  medium   -- syscall entry assembly + dispatch table
Stage 9:  small    -- wiring + test threads

Total: approximately 2000-3000 lines of new code across all stages.
